"""TagConnect TC2050-IDC-NL pad-only footprint wired as ARM Cortex 10-pin JTAG.

Uses KiCad's dual-mode `Conn_ARM_JTAG_SWD_10` symbol with the Tag-Connect
TC2050-IDC-NL 2x05 P1.27mm pad-only footprint, exposing the JTAG-mode signal
set (TMS/TCK/TDO/TDI/TRST + nRESET + VTRef + GND). No part is populated;
the cable mates directly to the pads.

Pin map (ARM Cortex Debug 10-pin connector, JTAG mode):
    1  VTref       7  KEY (no-connect, see note)
    2  TMS         8  TDI
    3  GND         9  nTRST (GNDDetect in symbol, see note)
    4  TCK        10  nRESET
    5  GND
    6  TDO

Notes:
- The KiCad `Conn_ARM_JTAG_SWD_10` symbol labels dual-mode pins by their
  SWD-mode names first (e.g. `SWDIO/TMS`); the JTAG-mode aliases are wired
  here.
- Pin 7 is `KEY` in the ARM Cortex Debug 10-pin spec and is hard-wired as
  a no-connect by the KiCad symbol. RTCK is not available on this connector;
  use a different debug header (e.g. ARM 20-pin) if RTCK is required.
- Pin 9 in the ARM Cortex Debug 10-pin spec is `GNDDetect` (target-present
  detect, often tied to GND on the probe). Several debuggers (e.g. SEGGER's
  9-pin Cortex-M adapter) repurpose it as nTRST in JTAG mode; we expose it
  as `JTAG.TRST` to match that convention. If your target needs pin 9 tied
  to GND for probe target-detect instead, wire `JTAG.TRST` to GND.
"""

load("@stdlib/interfaces.zen", "Jtag")

VTREF = io(Power(voltage="1.2V to 5.5V"))
GND = io(Ground)
JTAG = io(Jtag)
NRST = io(Net)

Component(
    name="J",
    symbol=Symbol(library="TagConnect.kicad_sym", name="Conn_ARM_JTAG_SWD_10"),
    pins={
        "VTref": VTREF,
        "GND": GND,
        "SWDIO/TMS": JTAG.TMS,
        "SWCLK/TCK": JTAG.TCK,
        "SWO/TDO": JTAG.TDO,
        "NC/TDI": JTAG.TDI,
        "GNDDetect": JTAG.TRST,
        "~{RESET}": NRST,
    },
    skip_bom=True,
    skip_pos=True,
)
